'''Bactrian''' (bak′tri-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''Bactrianus'' (Gr. Βακτριανός), < ''Bactria'', < Gr. Βακτρία (also Βάκτρα, < Pers. ''Bākhtar''), a province so called.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Of or pertaining to Bactria or Bactriana, an ancient country of central Asia, with its capital, Bactra, on the site of the modern Balkh. {{smaller|It became a province of the Persian empire under Cyrus, and from about 255 to about 126 {{sc|b. c.}} was a separate kingdom under a Greek dynasty.—'''Bactrian camel.''' See ''camel''.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A native or an inhabitant of Bactria.

'''Bactris''' (bak′tris), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. βάκτρον, a staff: see ''bacterium''.] A genus of slender palms, consisting of about 40 species, found about rivers and in marshy places in America within the tropics. {{smaller|The stems are generally covered}}

